import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  A guard written on an arm of a [case] or a [match] does not select: the guarded arm is taken
  whether or not its condition holds. @abs(x= -5)@ below answers @-5@, having run the arm guarded by
  @x2 >= 0@ on an input that is not.
    Print golden mismatch:
      expected: 5
      actual:   -5
  Both forms are wrong and identically so — @match(x)@ with @x2 | x2 >= 0=@ beside @x2 else =@ gives
  the same answer as the [case] below. Only the built-in [case]/[match] are affected; the same
  choice written as guards on the arms of a declared function is correct:
    abs(x -> Integer) -> Integer
    abs(x) | x >= 0= x
    abs(x) else = x * -1
  answers 5 for both 5 and -5. That is why @stack/rss/main.ct@ writes its branches that way.
  @test/Integration/code/case.ct@ and @match.ct@ do not catch this because each calls @abs(0)@ and
  nothing else, and at zero both arms answer zero. A test of a branch has to run the branch that is
  not taken.
  Not the same gap as @ifNonAgreeingBranches.ct@, though both are the declaration preprocessors in
  @Syntax/Ct/Desugarf/Preprocessors.hs@ rewriting an arm into a generated @$hash@ function. That one
  is a derived test failing on a program that runs correctly; this one is the program running
  wrongly and no test noticing.
abs(x -> Integer)= case(x)
  x2 | x2 >= 0= x2
  x2= x2 * -1

> abs(x= 5)
  >>> 5
> abs(x= -5)
  >>> 5
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
